  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
